WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.970
In this video you will build your own chart in
OurWater.

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:08.650
You will put a well and a rain gauge on one
picture, draw them your way,

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:11.290
and save the chart to open again.

00:00:12.117 --> 00:00:15.693
OurWater keeps its chart builder in the
Advanced menu.

00:00:15.813 --> 00:00:20.157
If you only see the short Easy menu, press
Advanced at the top.

00:00:20.983 --> 00:00:24.991
Then choose Advanced trend, under Advanced
analytics.

00:00:25.111 --> 00:00:27.127
The page opens empty.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:30.254
It starts empty on purpose.

00:00:30.374 --> 00:00:33.662
You build the chart one data series at a time.

00:00:33.782 --> 00:00:40.310
A data series is one station and one kind of
measurement, drawn as a line, bars or an area.

00:00:41.117 --> 00:00:43.421
Press Add data series.

00:00:43.541 --> 00:00:47.333
A row appears, already filled in with a first
choice.

00:00:47.453 --> 00:00:51.893
It has three parts: Station, Data type and
Chart type.

00:00:52.717 --> 00:00:54.805
Station comes first.

00:00:54.925 --> 00:00:59.725
Choose one station by name, or a whole area
such as Badgaon village.

00:00:59.845 --> 00:01:02.317
Here it is Asha's open well.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.486
Data type lists only what this station
records.

00:01:06.606 --> 00:01:09.822
For the well that is water level, in metres.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.810
The chart is drawn below.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:18.138
Time runs along the bottom, and the water
level, in metres, up the side.

00:01:18.258 --> 00:01:20.874
Each line you add gets its own colour.

00:01:21.683 --> 00:01:24.347
The strip under the chart lets you zoom.

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:27.899
Drag its two ends to look closer at one
season.

00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:31.309
Chart type is Time series here.

00:01:31.429 --> 00:01:36.901
Heatmap, Scatter, Distribution and 3D answer
other questions.

00:01:37.717 --> 00:01:40.069
Now add a second series.

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:45.133
Choose the Village rain gauge, and the data
type Rainfall, in millimetres.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:52.358
Water level is in metres and rain is in millimetres,
so OurWater puts the rain on its own scale,

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:54.350
on the right.

00:01:55.183 --> 00:01:58.135
Open the small arrow at the right of the row.

00:01:58.255 --> 00:02:06.079
Draw has four choices: Show as, Chart, Axis and Flip
Y axis, which turns the scale upside down.

00:02:06.883 --> 00:02:09.139
Axis decides the side.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:13.579
Put the rain on the left too, and the well's
line is squashed flat.

00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:16.255
Set it back to Right.

00:02:16.375 --> 00:02:20.407
Rain has its own scale again, and the well
keeps the left.

00:02:20.527 --> 00:02:23.479
Two kinds of measurement, one picture.

00:02:24.283 --> 00:02:28.651
Show as draws a series as a line, bars or an
area.

00:02:28.771 --> 00:02:31.051
Rain looks best as bars.

00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:37.115
Interval sets how often a point is drawn, from
every measurement to every year.

00:02:37.235 --> 00:02:40.211
Aggregate says how to combine each month.

00:02:40.331 --> 00:02:42.323
Sum adds up the rain.

00:02:42.443 --> 00:02:44.915
Average gives the typical value.

00:02:45.717 --> 00:02:48.285
Transform can smooth the wiggles.

00:02:48.405 --> 00:02:52.965
Normalize rescales a series, for example from
zero to one,

00:02:53.085 --> 00:02:56.733
so lines with different units can be compared
by shape.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:03.118
To compare two wells, add one more series: the
School borewell, water level.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.054
If a line does not help, press the cross at
the end of its row.

00:03:08.174 --> 00:03:10.790
That removes only that series.

00:03:11.617 --> 00:03:13.681
The borewell line is gone.

00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:16.297
Undo steps back one change.

00:03:16.417 --> 00:03:19.633
Reset clears every row and starts again.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.546
The time range covers every series at once.

00:03:23.666 --> 00:03:28.778
Drag the round handles, or use the arrow keys,
to look at a shorter period.

00:03:29.583 --> 00:03:31.455
Happy with the chart?

00:03:31.575 --> 00:03:34.191
Press Save view and give it a name.

00:03:34.311 --> 00:03:37.455
Here we call it Well and rain, monthly.

00:03:38.283 --> 00:03:40.155
Press Save.

00:03:40.275 --> 00:03:46.563
The chart is kept in Saved views, with its
rows, its axes, its bars and its dates.

00:03:47.383 --> 00:03:50.071
Now press Reset to clear the page.

00:03:50.191 --> 00:03:53.623
Everything goes, but the saved view is safe.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.922
Choose it from the Saved views list.

00:03:57.042 --> 00:04:02.178
The two series, the right axis, the bars and
the dates all come back.

00:04:02.983 --> 00:04:06.247
Set default opens this view for you next time.

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:10.399
Delete removes it straight away, so check
before you press it.

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:16.233
This page has no download button, so a saved
view is how you keep the chart.

00:04:16.353 --> 00:04:22.041
To take the numbers away, open a station's
measurements and press Export for an Excel file.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:25.826
A new station may have no measurements yet.

00:04:25.946 --> 00:04:30.242
The page then says so, instead of drawing an
empty chart.

00:04:30.362 --> 00:04:33.050
Pick another station in the row above.

00:04:33.883 --> 00:04:38.755
Now you can build a chart one series at a
time, give rain its own axis,

00:04:38.875 --> 00:04:41.563
and keep the picture as a saved view.

00:04:41.683 --> 00:04:44.347
Thanks for helping look after our water.
